1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:04,110
Packing for a journey to the moon is kind of like any other road trip.

2
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:09,110
You need clothes, food, a map, your favorite toy, really.

3
00:00:09,110 --> 00:00:14,110
Except this toy has a job to do up there because this is a zero gravity indicator.

4
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:21,110
While not a scientific instrument, this safe and soft object tells the strapped-in astronauts that they've reached weightlessness.

5
00:00:21,110 --> 00:00:28,110
For Artemis II, the crew wanted a one-of-a-kind ZGI, so we asked you to come up with it.

6
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:40,350
Thanks everyone for sending in your entries from around the world. Over 2,600 of you submitted.

7
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:48,350
And we loved seeing all of your contributions. It was a really difficult decision because each design had a unique quality that stood out to our crew.

8
00:00:48,350 --> 00:00:58,350
In the end we were able to narrow it down to a top five from Finland, Canada, Peru, and two from the United States, Kansas and California.

9
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:13,300
In the end we went with one called RISE. It was a design inspired by the Earthrise photo taken on Apollo 8, which is very special to our crew.

10
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:21,300
With the winner chosen, next came the task of bringing it to life, here in the Thermal Blanket Lab at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center.

11
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:31,300
This lab specializes in custom materials to protect sensitive spacecraft equipment and is perfect for creating a ZGI that meets the rigorous safety standards of NASA.

12
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:38,300
Because you have to be concerned with whether the bread's going to outcast or if it will hold up if it's flame retardant.

13
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:50,300
This is the RISE ZGI doll. All I have left to do is to close the opening here in hand so the hat on, make sure it's stuffing is stuffed in there.

14
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:54,300
And we have our pocket here for the SD card.

15
00:01:54,300 --> 00:02:01,300
And on that tiny SD card will be millions of names submitted to virtually join the crew aboard Orion.

